# Encoded Email Addresses

Email addresses on this site are never written into the HTML in plain form —
harvesters scrape page source. They ship XOR-encoded in the markup and are
decoded in the visitor's browser by `/engine/email-decode.js`, so the visitor
sees the real address but `view-source` never contains it.

This replicates Cloudflare Scrape Shield "Email Obfuscation", which the old
proxied site relied on. Off that proxy, Cloudflare's injected
`/cdn-cgi/scripts/.../email-decode.min.js` 404s and the page shows the literal
`[email protected]` fallback — that is the symptom this technique fixes.

## Encoding scheme

A hex string. The first byte is the XOR key; each later byte XORed with the
key is one byte of the UTF-8 address.

Encode one-liner (address → hex, key `0x2a` arbitrary):

```js
const enc = (s, k = 0x2a) =>
  [k, ...new TextEncoder().encode(s).map((b) => b ^ k)]
    .map((b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');
```

## Markup shapes (both handled by the decoder)

1. **Display text** — a span the decoder replaces with the decoded address:

   ```html
   <span class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="HEX">[email&#160;protected]</span>
   ```

2. **Mailto link** — an anchor whose href fragment decodes to the mailto
   target (a `?subject=` suffix survives into the `mailto:`). If the anchor
   itself carries `data-cfemail`, its text is replaced too:

   ```html
   <a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection#HEX">…</a>
   <a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="HEX">[email&#160;protected]</a>
   ```

The `[email&#160;protected]` inner text is the no-JS fallback — keep it.

## To add an encoded email to a page

1. Encode the address (and for links, the full target including any
   `?subject=` suffix) with the one-liner above.
2. Use one of the markup shapes.
3. Include the decoder before the closing body scripts:
   `<script src="/engine/email-decode.js"></script>`

Used on: `web/src/html/main.contact.html`, `web/src/html/main.about.html`.
Never swap the markup for a plain `mailto:` or paste the decoded address into
any served HTML — that defeats the whole mechanism.
